WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.320
Hey Wisecrack, disembodied Jared here, grab
that potato chip and eat it, because we’re

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.140
talking about the anime phenomenon - Death
Note.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:21.699
While Death Note addresses a range of philosophical
topics, spanning from property rights, to

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:26.180
human nature, one of the most interesting
is Justice— what is it, and how should it

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.570
be applied?

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.150
Justice is hardly a new topic in media.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.469
So why is Death Note so special?

00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:37.469
Well, whereas most pop culture gives us a
cut and dry idea of justice Death Note is

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:42.139
unique not in WHAT it says about justice,
but how it goes about saying it.

00:00:42.139 --> 00:00:45.429
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy
of Death Note.

00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:48.789
And, even though this show is ten years old—
spoilers ahead.

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:53.360
Also a quick note about dubs vs. subs- Seems
like no matter what we do, we somehow do it

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:54.360
wrong.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.059
So we’re just going to use the dubs on this
one because it’s easier for us to work with

00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:00.289
- hey, that’s more time to read and write
and make more episodes.

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:02.129
So please don’t hate us.

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:07.250
Death Note follows Light Yagami as he discovers
the ...Death Note a notebook that can kill

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.229
anyone by writing their name in it and picturing
their face.

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:15.570
Light, a model student, wants to use the Death
Note to create a world devoid of crime.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:20.210
Soon after, he adopts the public persona of
“Kira,” the God of the new world he hopes

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:21.210
to create.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:25.880
Of course, anonymously killing huge swaths
of criminals places him in conflict with various

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:31.920
law enforcement agencies, a mysterious detective
named L and his two successors: M and N. In

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.960
Death Note, we see multiple characters exploring
what is and isn’t Justice.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.000
Let’s just say the word comes up a lot.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.400
"I am justice."

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.200
"Justice."

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.200
"Justice."

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.580
"Justice."

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:48.020
But the cool thing is, the show doesn’t
give us a clear stance on what constitutes

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:54.300
justice, but rather, employs a unique method
to constantly challenge various definitions of it.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:59.440
An element of that method happens in how individual
characters propose their own version of justice.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.439
And first among these is Light himself.

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:05.719
It’s hard to get a good sense of who Light
was prior to the Death Note, although we have

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.460
some clues to his moral compass

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.320
"There must be something wrong with me to even consider it."

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.120
and his sense
of moral obligation,

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.800
"This world is rotting and those who are making it rot deserve to die."

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.800
"Someone has to do it so why not me."

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.860
Kira is an ends-justify-the-means kinda guy.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:26.040
Essentially, the ends of being a crime-zapping
uber-tyrant justify pretty much anything,

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.800
including shamelessly using his girlfriend
to distract the police and maximize his kill count

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:37.160
"I wish I knew but I don't. Because if I did, I'll thank him for punishing the burglar who killed my parents.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.980
To me, Kira is a hero."

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.020
and killing people who aren’t even criminals

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.460
who get in his way.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.840
However, once Light renounces ownership of
the notebook.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.460
"I hereby forfeit ownership of this notebook."

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:53.600
He loses his memories of being Kira, his personality
changes noticeably, as does his sense of justice.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.549
He goes from a “by any means necessary”
kinda dude to a “well maybe I shouldn’t

00:02:58.549 --> 00:03:00.349
always be an asshole” kinda dude.

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:04.460
This is shown by his refusal to even use Misa’s
feelings in order to get information.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.540
"To me, exploiting another person's feelings for personal gain is unforgivable

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.540
and I won't allow myself to stoop that low."

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.060
This is a huge change from the guy who pretends
to love her for years just because it was

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.840
convenient for killing people.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.220
Light is even told by Misa that she doesn’t
mind being used for his cause.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.900
"I already told you I don't care, even if all you do is use me"

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.680
You’d think that would be enough, but he
always goes an extra step out of the way to

00:03:27.689 --> 00:03:29.260
break his promises to her.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.110
Kira’s a bit of a fuckboy.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.660
But Light, minus the notebook, seems to respect
Misa at least enough to not lead her on.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.740
"Hey Light, wanna come sleep with me tonight?

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.720
"Wh-what are you talking about?"

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:45.980
Once he touches the Death Note again and regains
his memories and powers his Kira persona returns,

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:50.310
and he moves in with Misa almost immediately
to get even more bonus killing power.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:55.720
In other words, “treating people well”
is thrown out the window for Justice with a capital J

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:00.620
There are countless examples of this tension
between Light pre and post-Notebook, although

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:03.580
they merge more and more toward the end of
the show.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:07.330
One of the ways this is portrayed is through
Light’s conversations with himself, where

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:10.209
he grapples with moral and practical dilemmas.

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:12.160
Does that split personality sound familiar?

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.300
"Oh, yes, we was, precious. We was!"

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.700
Both Death Note and Lord of the Rings are
likely descendants of an ancient Greek story

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:21.590
called The Ring of Gyges.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:27.110
A guy finds a ring that makes him invisible,
and using this anonymity, he immediately bangs

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:32.740
the King’s wife, kills the king, and takes
over whatever spooky Greek magic town he lives in.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:36.800
We could probably read this as an allegory
for the perils of internet comment sections

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.640
but I digress.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.670
In Plato’s Republic, this story raises a
lot of questions about the idea of justice.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:48.630
The character Glaucon, who was actually Plato’s
real-life big brother, uses the fable to argue

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:53.450
that Justice is just a kind of social contract
that keeps people from hurting each other.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:57.500
The thought experiment is meant to demonstrate
that people only really value justice for

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.080
its ability to make them look good.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:03.530
In other words, it’s only natural that someone
with an invisibility ring would go around

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:08.260
acting like an asshat, because people only
value justice or morality when their actions

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:09.620
can be scrutinized.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:10.680
Sound familiar?

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.380
"Humans always try to maintain appearances when they're in public. That's just how we are.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:21.220
But this is how they really feel. Most are too afraid to support me as they're worried about what others will think.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:28.080
Many would rather deny my existence, but on the internet, where you can remain anonymous, support for Kira is growing."

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:33.440
As soon as the risk of damaging your reputation
is gone, so is all your interest in justice.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:37.970
Glaucon goes on to say that it doesn’t matter
if you were just or unjust before putting

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:43.080
on the ring— the power will inevitably lead
you to be a big bag of dicks, and that’s okay!

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.480
"I can use the Death Note all I want and I won't be punished?"

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.340
According to Glaucon, if you can secretly
bang the queen and still keep your reputation,

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:55.420
you will be way happier than someone who cares
about justice and bangs zero queens.

00:05:55.430 --> 00:06:00.660
And that’s pretty much Light: the golden
boy valedictorian who puts on a show of “normal”

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.500
morality, even while he takes joy in doing
stuff that is probably way worse than breaking

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:07.120
the royal bro code.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:12.200
"I never once felt cursed since I picked up the Death Note. In fact, the thought never even crossed my mind.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:19.940
I'm happier than I've ever been all thanks to this power. I'm going to create a perfect world."

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.780
So why do Glaucon’s musings matter here?

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:27.360
Because one of the key questions Death Note
asks is: “What does Justice mean when nobody's

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:28.360
looking?”

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:33.870
This theme is repeated in Light’s pseudonymity
as Kira, in L and N’s namelessness, and

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.640
in the anonymity of the Kira supporters later
in the show.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:39.590
And here’s where the Republic really comes
in handy.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:44.400
As a Socratic Dialogue, it’s far less concerned
with offering a theory of justice than it

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.580
is with detailing the process of exploring
justice.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:53.610
In it, Socrates and his frenemies use hypothetical
scenarios and varying definitions to challenge

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:55.590
what constitutes justice.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:07:00.560
Death Note works in a similar way, showing
the movement and evolution of theories of

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:03.460
justice between and within the characters.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:07.901
Many of the characters in Death Note— Misa,
Light, and Mikami, to name a few— believe

00:07:07.901 --> 00:07:12.430
that they possess true knowledge of justice,
and the tension between these characters and

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:15.380
their skeptics draws out the real meat of
the show.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:19.660
In one of the least-subtle scenes, both L
and Light declare in unison.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:23.060
"I am justice."

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:27.860
This sentiment is repeated throughout the
series, especially in the final episode, where

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:33.240
Light claims that by reducing violent crime
and ending wars, he has become justice itself.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:39.020
"Kira has become law in the world we now live. He's the one who's maintaining order.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.520
I have become justice."

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.190
And here Death Note establishes itself as
a kind of modern Socratic Dialogue - using

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:51.360
multiple participants to try to flesh out
some universal question like: “What is Justice”--

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:52.860
a la The Republic.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.700
From the minute Light proposes his vision
of a new world in Episode 1,

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:03.060
"I want the world to know of my existence. That there's someone out there passing righteous judgment on the wicked."

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.720
the question of justice is brought into play.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.820
While he does struggle with himself for a
second,

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.000
"Who am I to pass judgment on others?"

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.320
his first real challenger is Ryuk.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:17.060
Light sets out his plan to immediately kill off the worst criminals, while letting the lesser

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:18.120
ones die off.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:22.120
"Then and only then the world will start moving in the right direction.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:29.240
It'll be a new world, free of injustice and populated by people who I've judged to be honest, kind, and hard working."

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.280
"But if you did that, it would make you the only bad person left."

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:38.620
Ryuk is motivated by boredom rather than any
sense of moral duty, but he notices the contradiction

00:08:38.629 --> 00:08:42.779
in Light’s argument, beginning the Socratic
Dialogue that lasts the rest of the show.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:47.649
There’s no need to chronicle every moment
of this series-long philosophical inquiry.

00:08:47.649 --> 00:08:51.580
But there are definitely some highlights that
show us the trajectory of this conversation.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:56.500
"I've got a pretty good idea what your motivation might be and I can guess what you hope to achieve.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:00.740
However, what you're doing right now is evil."

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:04.520
"You think I'm evil? I am justice!"

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:08.800
If this wasn’t clear enough, this is where
they end the episode by yelling “I am Justice!”

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:11.170
just to make sure we get it.

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:12.170
And did we get it?

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:14.930
Right— they both think they’re Justice.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.360
Light likes to refer to himself as a martyr

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.080
"Even if I have to sacrifice my own mind and soul, it's worth it."

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:23.080
which is pretty weird considering that he

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.050
spends years trying to make himself the ruler
of the entire world.

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:31.920
But if you subscribe to his quasi-utilitarian
theory of justice— that killing criminals

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.850
and making one person God will ensure peace
and happiness for the greatest number of people

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:42.240
then he is sacrificing himself, knowing that
becoming the god of the new world will mean

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:46.800
becoming more and more Kira, slowly killing
Light Yagami.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:51.600
It also means that in exchange for cleaning
a rotten world, he would have to contend with

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.660
a rotten self, something that he acknowledges.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:10:02.180
"Kira probably knows that what he's doing is evil. But he'll try to change the world even as a martyr at the cost of his soul.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.180
That's his brand of justice."

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:08.871
So this is Light’s conception of justice—
the world needs a Kira, and since

00:10:08.871 --> 00:10:13.540
he was chosen, he's willing to sacrifice
himself in order to make that happen.

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:19.199
Kira’s idea of justice is an Old Testament-style
bloodbath where sinners get punished and blasphemy

00:10:19.199 --> 00:10:20.740
is the biggest sin of all.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:24.310
Did we mention the veritable monsoon of Biblical
imagery in the show?

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:32.380
You know, the churchy choral music, this ode
to to Michelangelo’s “The Creation of Adam”,

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:35.540
the apples everywhere, L even washes Light’s feet.

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:39.750
Since Light spends most of the show acting
as Kira, the distinction between the two may

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:41.720
not be terribly important.

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:46.660
But Kira seems to represent Light’s ideal
version of justice: that the only true just

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:52.279
world is one without criminals, achieved by
whatever means necessary.

00:10:52.279 --> 00:10:58.019
However - L, speaking as Lind L. Tailor, highlights
the contradictions in Kira’s reasoning.

00:10:58.019 --> 00:11:03.249
If a just world is one without criminality,
then the criminal act of killing people is

00:11:03.249 --> 00:11:04.700
also an injustice.

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:09.110
This resembles Ryuk’s rebuttal in Episode
1, but the difference is that Ryuk stops at

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:14.279
the suggestion that Light might be “bad,”
while L offers a competing example. Not only

00:11:14.279 --> 00:11:18.829
is Kira unjust; catching Kira is itself an
act of Justice.

00:11:18.829 --> 00:11:23.209
The show, stops short of telling us exactly
what L believes Justice is, in order to believe

00:11:23.209 --> 00:11:24.860
that Kira is violating it.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:29.209
But that’s kind of how Socratic Dialogues
work - poking holes and raising questions

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:31.029
to get closer to the truth.

00:11:31.029 --> 00:11:35.280
Kira imagines justice as a grand ideal - a
world without criminals.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:39.380
L, however, only talks about justice as a
reaction to injustice.

00:11:39.389 --> 00:11:44.680
A lot of characters believe in Light’s ideal
justice but others seem to echo L.

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:50.149
Philosopher John Rawls, much like Light and
Plato, argues that in order to correct injustices

00:11:50.149 --> 00:11:54.550
in society, we need to first have an idea
of what “justice” is.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:59.180
Rawls and Plato disagree on a lot, but they
both support “ideal theory”-- that when

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:03.019
thinking about Justice, we should first try
to understand what it would look like in a

00:12:03.019 --> 00:12:07.059
perfect world where everyone did exactly the
right thing.

00:12:07.059 --> 00:12:12.269
This is justice under “ideal” conditions,
which for Light and Mikami, involve a lot

00:12:12.269 --> 00:12:14.060
of people being dead.

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:19.769
However, others, like philosopher Naomi Zack,
favor something called “injustice theory.”

00:12:19.769 --> 00:12:24.680
This approach does not rely on a preexisting
ideal of “justice” and the perfect just

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:28.650
society but rather centers on identifying
injustices where they occur.

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:34.660
It is much more practical to point to a case
of injustice than it is to start from a broadly-applicable

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:36.550
definition of what is just.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:41.240
By this measure, only after injustice has
been identified can justice be applied to

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:42.240
correct it.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:47.259
The L-M-N-alphabet-soup’s hunt for Kira
doesn’t seem to be directed by a belief

00:12:47.259 --> 00:12:51.860
in a high-in-the-sky definition of Justice
but rather by the recognition that Kira’s

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.089
“new world” is unjust.

00:12:54.089 --> 00:12:58.889
Since we never get a clear sense of what L’s
concept of “justice” is, it seems totally

00:12:58.889 --> 00:13:02.709
plausible that he sees justice as the response
to injustice.

00:13:02.709 --> 00:13:08.110
So, while Light and those similar to him favor
a Rawlsian approach of a world created around

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:13.710
a perfect “Justice,” L is more focused
on correcting injustices as they appear; any

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:17.210
definition of The Just becomes secondary.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:21.560
The struggle— and resulting dialogue—
between these beliefs characterizes much of

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:25.889
the philosophical method in the show, although
other characters like Matsuda complicate the

00:13:25.889 --> 00:13:30.189
conversation and prevent it from being asplit
between just two ideas.

00:13:30.189 --> 00:13:34.720
That being said, we should briefly take a
second look at our cake-snarfing Sherlock Holmes.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:39.240
His code of justice is a bit more ambiguous
than Light’s, although its very ambiguity

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.139
plays an important role.

00:13:41.139 --> 00:13:46.100
In many cases, it appears that L, and N, as
well as M, are most interested in winning

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.589
a game of wits.

00:13:47.589 --> 00:13:51.680
That “game” may be a symbolic battle between
competing moralities, or it could just be

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:55.410
a case of some narcissistic geniuses obsessed
with puzzles.

00:13:55.410 --> 00:14:00.439
In either case, this lack of any strong convictions
about a positive meaning of Justice serves

00:14:00.439 --> 00:14:02.360
at least one critical purpose.

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:07.040
It means that L frequently plays the role
of inquisitor, not so different from Socrates

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:08.070
in The Republic.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:13.040
Another, somewhat more surprising inquisitor
is Matsuda, who raises sincere questions about

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:18.110
the moral judgments of Light, Kira, the Japanese
Police, L… just about everyone, even at

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:21.709
the same time as he is mocked for his inability
to fall into line.

00:14:21.709 --> 00:14:26.361
Early in the season, he responds to L’s
speech on the childish morality of Kira by

00:14:26.361 --> 00:14:30.660
pointing out that violent crime in the area
had decreased since Kira began killing.

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:37.040
"Well I'm not saying this to support Kira or condone the murders but in the last few days, throughout the world,

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:43.320
but especially here in Japan, we've observed a dramatic decrease in the number of violent crimes committed."

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:48.780
He makes clear that he does not condone Kira’s
vision or actions, but he is also skeptical

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:53.069
of the hard line taken by L. Later in the
series, he has an exchange with the other

00:14:53.069 --> 00:14:57.730
members of the Task Force that calls into
question the very foundation of the squad:

00:14:57.730 --> 00:15:00.160
the belief in the inherent evilness of Kira.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:03.320
"Do you guys ever think that maybe Kira isn't completely evil?"

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:06.400
"What?"
"What kind of question is that Matsuda?"

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:10.600
"I've been thinking about it and part of me just doesn't believe that he is."

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.740
"So now you think Kira is justice too?"

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:19.680
"I don't really know, but I think that Kira's trying to fight evil and change the world in his own way

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:24.280
and well the worlds become a good place for people who live honest lives so..."

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:29.420
Matsuda, with all his self-doubt, is both
audience surrogate and philosophical foil

00:15:29.429 --> 00:15:31.079
to the other characters.

00:15:31.079 --> 00:15:35.660
Unlike Light, who is convinced of his own
project, L and N, who are single-mindedly

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:40.500
obsessed with the task of catching him, Chief
Yagami, who is focused on finding the “true”

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:45.069
Kira to clear his son’s name, and the rest
of the Task Force, who have staked their lives

00:15:45.069 --> 00:15:50.230
and beliefs on the Justice of ending Kira
once and for all, Matsuda is constantly questioning

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:52.760
the values that are presented to him as fact.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:57.620
"Hang on, am I the only one who sees something wrong? He can go around accusing people of being Kira

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:02.080
but the fact that he's wearing a mask says he's only trying to protect himself, not anyone else."

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:06.840
Matsuda’s demeanor is very different than
the bravado of Plato’s debate club boys,

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:09.840
but he first and foremost recognizes his own
ignorance,

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:11.840
"It is? Uh- I mean, no way!"

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:13.340
which is crucial for revealing

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:15.380
the ignorance of those around him.

00:16:15.389 --> 00:16:19.960
Even at the end, as he kills Light, he does
so with questions, rather than answers.

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:25.440
"What was it all for then? What about your dad? What the hell did he die for?"

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:30.139
By forcing the viewers and the other characters
to consider their own opinions of justice,

00:16:30.139 --> 00:16:36.019
Matsuda helps to organize a jumble of competing
ideas into a somewhat-intelligible conversation.

00:16:36.019 --> 00:16:38.259
Matsuda the modern Socrates!

00:16:38.259 --> 00:16:43.339
As ten years of fan forums demonstrate, there
is no real consensus on who best represented

00:16:43.339 --> 00:16:47.110
Justice or whether Light’s “New World”
justified the means.

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:50.500
But that ambiguity is what makes the show
so poignant.

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:55.420
Death Note is an addicting detective procedural,
but it never abandons its original question

00:16:55.420 --> 00:17:01.079
of what makes a just world, and most importantly,
it never gives us an answer.

00:17:01.079 --> 00:17:06.140
In Episode 30, aptly named “Justice,”
Light pretty much sums it up: "If Kira gets

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:08.250
caught then that makes him evil.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.840
But if he wins and rules the world then I guess he's
justice."

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:15.490
By the end of the show, Kira is defeated and
N’s brand of justice seems to prevail.

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:19.610
But it leaves us with a feeling that things
might have been very different if Kira had

00:17:19.610 --> 00:17:21.360
survived the warehouse.

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:26.420
Rather than giving us closure that L and N
were innately more just than Kira, this idea

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:31.430
of winner-gets-all morality reveals that the
war of competing moral codes was a kind of

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:34.530
logic game, not a kind of truth-seeking.

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:37.820
You could argue that this makes “justice”
totally meaningless.

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:41.280
For the world inside the show, N might be
justice.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:44.820
But for us, the viewers, it’s not quite
so clear cut.

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:49.270
In regards to the meaning and implications
of justice, Death Note opens more doors than

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:50.320
it closes.

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:55.380
In terms of whether or not Socrates… or
Rawls… or Zack would find L or Kira to be

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:59.130
paragons of justice— the short answer is,
definitely not.

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:02.070
Although there are some other similarities
that we didn’t address in this video—

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:06.680
like themes of tyranny and the focus on what
makes a “just city”— the real connection

00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:11.890
between the Justice of The Republic and justice
in Death Note is not in their definitions

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:13.630
but in their methods.

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:18.700
And that method distinguishes Death Note as
a show that does philosophy rather than just

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:49.240
borrowing it.

00:18:49.240 --> 00:19:44.290
Thanks for watching, guys.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:56.650
Peace.

